{
  "problem_statement": "*** Problem Statement *** \n\n# Steinberg Variety for Hyperspherical Varieties\n\n## Background\n\nLet $G$ be a reductive group over $\\mathbb{C}$.\n\n**Definition.** A *$G$-hyperspherical variety* $M$ is a smooth affine $G$-Hamiltonian variety satisfying:\n\n1. The space $\\mathbb{C}[M]^G$ of $G$-invariant rational functions on $M$ is commutative under the Poisson bracket induced by the symplectic structure on $M$.\n2. The image of the moment map $\\mu \\colon M \\to \\mathfrak{g}^*$ intersects the nilcone $\\mathcal{N}$ of $\\mathfrak{g}^*$.\n3. The stabilizer of a generic point in $M$ is connected.\n\n**Examples:**\n- If $M = T^*X$ is the cotangent bundle of a smooth $G$-variety $X$, then $M$ is hyperspherical iff $X$ is spherical.\n- If $M = W$ is a symplectic representation of $G$, then $M$ is hyperspherical iff $W$ is a multiplicity-free symplectic representation (classified by Knop [MR2236756]).\n\nLet $\\pi \\colon \\widetilde{\\mathcal{N}} = T^*(\\mathcal{B}) \\to \\mathcal{N}$ be the Springer resolution of the nilcone $\\mathcal{N}$, where $\\mathcal{B}$ is the flag variety of $G$. The *Steinberg variety* for a hyperspherical variety $M$ is\n\n$$\\mathrm{St}_M := M \\times_{\\mathfrak{g}^*} \\widetilde{\\mathcal{N}}.$$\n\n**BFT Conjecture .** $\\mathrm{St}_M$ is a Lagrangian subvariety of $M \\times \\widetilde{\\mathcal{N}}$.\n\n## Cotangent bundle case: $M = T^*X$\n\nWhen $M = T^*X$ for a smooth $G$-variety $X$,\n\n$$\\mathrm{St}_M = \\bigsqcup_Z T_Z^*(X \\times \\mathcal{B}),$$\n\nwhere $Z$ runs over all $G$-orbits in $X \\times \\mathcal{B}$. Finiteness of the number of $G$-orbits follows from the fact that commutativity of $\\mathbb{C}[M]^G$ under the Poisson bracket is equivalent to commutativity of $D(X)^G$ under the usual product, which implies $\\mathbb{C}[X]$ is multiplicity-free as a $G$-module, hence $B$ has an open orbit in $X$, and thus $X$ has finitely many $B$-orbits.\n\n---\n\n## Symplectic representation case: $M = W$\n\n**Task:** Generalize the above argument to symplectic representations.\n\nLet $M = W$ be a symplectic representation of $G$ and $\\mathcal{W}(W)$ the Weyl algebra of $W$. Commutativity of $\\mathbb{C}[M]^G$ under the Poisson bracket is equivalent to commutativity of $\\mathcal{W}(W)^G$ under the usual product.\n\n$G$ acts on the set $\\mathcal{L}(W)$ of Lagrangians of $W$. Fix a closed orbit $\\mathcal{O}$ in $\\mathcal{L}(W)$ and $X \\in \\mathcal{O}$. Let $P = \\mathrm{Stab}_G(X)$. Then $P$ is a parabolic subgroup with unipotent radical $U$ and Levi quotient $\\iota \\colon P \\to L = P/U$. Denote the Lie algebras by $\\mathfrak{p}, \\mathfrak{u}, \\mathfrak{l}$.\n\nChoose a Borel subgroup $B \\subseteq P$ with Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{b}$. Let $\\overline{B} = \\iota(B)$ and $\\overline{\\mathfrak{b}} = \\mathrm{Lie}(\\overline{B})$, so $\\overline{B}$ is a Borel subgroup of $L$.\n\nWe have an exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow \\mathfrak{u} \\longrightarrow \\mathfrak{b} \\longrightarrow \\overline{\\mathfrak{b}} \\longrightarrow 0.$$\n\nTake a complete polarization $W = X \\oplus Y$ and identify $W$ with $T^*Y$ via the projection $\\Pi \\colon W \\to Y$. Then\n\n$$\\mathfrak{b} = \\mathfrak{u} \\overline{\\mathfrak{b}}, \\qquad \\mathfrak{b}^\\perp = \\overline{\\mathfrak{b}}^\\perp \\mathfrak{u}^\\perp,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathfrak{b}^\\perp$ is the annihilator of $\\mathfrak{b}$ in $\\mathfrak{g}^*$, and similarly for the others.\n\n**Equivalent BFT conjecture.** The BFT conjecture is equivalent to:\n\n$$\\Lambda_W = \\mu^{-1}(\\mathfrak{b}^\\perp) \\subset W \\text{ is a Lagrangian.}$$\n\n**Definition.** The *moment cone* is\n\n$$\\mathcal{N}_Y = \\{ y \\in Y \\mid \\langle u y, y \\rangle = 0,\\; \\forall u \\in \\mathfrak{u} \\}.$$\n\n$\\mathcal{N}_Y$ is $L$-invariant. One can show:\n\n$$\\mu^{-1}(\\mathfrak{u}^\\perp) = \\Pi^{-1}(\\mathcal{N}_Y).$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\\Lambda_W = \\mu^{-1}(\\mathfrak{u}^\\perp) \\cap \\mu^{-1}(\\overline{\\mathfrak{b}}^\\perp) = \\bigsqcup_Z T_Z^*(Y),$$\n\nwhere $Z$ runs over all $\\overline{B}$-orbits in $\\mathcal{N}_Y$. So the conjecture reduces to finiteness of the number of $\\overline{B}$-orbits in $\\mathcal{N}_Y$ (i.e., $\\mathcal{N}_Y$ is spherical for $\\overline{B}$, ignoring normality; note $\\mathcal{N}_Y$ is singular).\n\n**Strategy.** Relate $\\mathcal{W}(W)^G = \\mathcal{D}(Y)^G$ to $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{N}_Y)^L$ (where $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{N}_Y)$ is defined using the embedding $\\mathcal{N}_Y \\subset Y$ and $\\mathcal{D}(Y)$):\n\n1. Compute $\\mathcal{D}(Y)^U$ or $\\mathcal{D}(Y)^{\\overline{U}}$ and show that $U$- or $\\overline{U}$-invariance implies the support of the differential operator lies in $\\mathcal{N}_Y$.\n2. Show $\\mathcal{D}(Y)^G = (\\mathcal{D}(Y)^U \\cap \\mathcal{D}(Y)^{\\overline{U}})^L$ is isomorphic to $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{N}_Y)^L$.\n3. If $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{N}_Y)^L$ is commutative, then $\\mathcal{N}_Y$ is spherical for $L$, hence finitely many $\\overline{B}$-orbits in $\\mathcal{N}_Y$.\n\n## Problem\n\n**Carry out the above strategy or otherwise to prove the equivalent BFT conjecture for symplectic representations.**\n\n(Note: $\\mathcal{N}_Y$ is singular; the definition of $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{N}_Y)$ may require further clarification.)\n",
